 Pseudo-Transcript: A.2 Shared Module Library Discussion

  USER: look at /TODO.md, let's discuss A.2 after you explore the code base

  CLAUDE: Read TODO.md, then launched an exploration agent to find all UART and I2C
  implementations. Found 3 copies of uart_tx across projects (two identical, one
  with reset support), 2 copies of i2c_master. Presented a summary table and
  identified 4 key decisions to make: interface naming, reset support, which version
   to canonicalize, and whether to include I2C in library.

  USER: 2-3, I want reset support and logic implementation. 4. this project is
  iCEUP5K specific, so lattice-specific is fine. give me recommendations on 1 from
  an industry perspective

  CLAUDE: Recommended ready_o over busy_o. Reasoning: AXI-Stream uses TREADY/TVALID,
   positive logic reads more naturally in code, Xilinx/Intel IP cores use ready,
  FIFO conventions use ready/valid. Existing busy_o isn't wrong but ready_o aligns
  better with industry standards for a canonical library.

  USER: yes (to proceeding with plan)

  CLAUDE: Launched a Plan agent to design the implementation. Created detailed plan
  with file list, interface specifications, implementation steps. Wrote plan to plan
   file and called ExitPlanMode.

  USER: why are uart_rx, spi_master, debounce future modules

  CLAUDE: Explained that TODO.md itself listed these as "(future)" because no
  implementations exist yet in the codebase. uart_rx has no reusable module,
  spi_master doesn't exist standalone, debounce is listed as "after Project B".
  Asked if user wants to expand scope to include any of these now.

  USER: I had a lot of problems with dac-adc-loopback/ getting it to work. it seemed
   simple to me that the adc-read and dac-ramp are almost independent projects that
  combining should be easy but it took a lot of debugging and I don't know why. take
   a good look at all three projects and determine if using dac-adc-loopback as a
  reference for the library is a good idea

  CLAUDE: Launched thorough analysis of all three projects. Found key issues:
  1. SPI timing mismatch - DAC captures on falling edge, ADC outputs on falling edge
  2. The bit-shift bug - code extracts [12:1] instead of [11:0] to compensate for
  missing first bit
  3. Clock domain simplification - loopback removed 60MHz PLL, dropped to 6MHz SPI

  Recommendation: Don't use dac-adc-loopback as library reference. The [12:1]
  extraction is a band-aid fix. Proceed with uart_tx and i2c_master from
  adc-read-i2c-reset-logic, leave SPI as device-specific for now.

  USER: what about making part specific library, like the ads1115, the dac and the
  adc used in the spi projects. thoughts

  CLAUDE: Proposed hybrid two-tier architecture:
  - Protocol level (universal): uart_tx, uart_rx, i2c_master, spi_master
  - Device level (part-specific): ads1115, ad7476a, dac121s101

  Benefits: Clean APIs like ads1115_read(channel), quirks hidden inside wrappers,
  still educational since users can read device modules to learn protocols.

  Asked if user wants all device modules in initial library or start smaller.

  USER: what about uart-rx? why isn't this in the list?

  CLAUDE: Searched codebase, found src/uart-rx/uart_rx.v exists but it's a demo
  module with LED output, not a reusable data interface. The existing module
  controls an LED based on '0'/'1' keypresses - received byte processing is baked
  in. A reusable version needs data_o and valid_o ports instead of LED. Added
  uart_rx to the library proposal with note to extract and refactor from demo.

  USER: I want a spi_master.v, it just seems logical. can you fix it based on
  comments here to be a generic functional spi_master.v

  CLAUDE: Yes, explained the root cause of AD7476A bug - in CPHA=0 mode, slave
  outputs data when CS falls BEFORE any clock edge, but existing code creates
  falling edge first then samples, missing the first bit.

  Proposed generic interface with CPOL, CPHA, WIDTH, HALF_PERIOD parameters. Device
  modules would wrap this with correct settings - ad7476a uses CPOL=0 CPHA=0,
  dac121s101 uses CPOL=0 CPHA=1.

  USER: I want you update the /TODO.md section A.2 with all this information and
  decisions that we made here for another agent to implement.

  CLAUDE: Updated TODO.md A.2 section from ~45 lines to ~300 lines with:
  - Two-tier architecture explanation
  - Interface conventions table
  - Source files reference with warnings about buggy code
  - Complete interface specs for all 7 modules
  - SPI mode truth table and bug explanation
  - Implementation steps
  - Basic verification approach

  USER: commit and push

  CLAUDE: Staged TODO.md specifically (not git add -A), committed with descriptive
  message about expanding A.2 spec, pushed to main. Commit 3a1d0e0.

  USER: how do we test these new library components?

  CLAUDE: Presented three levels of testing:
  1. Synthesis check - yosys, catches syntax errors only
  2. Simulation with testbenches - iverilog, critical for proving SPI fix works
  3. Hardware verification - lib-test project with loopback tests

  Recommended starting with testbenches for protocol modules, especially
  spi_master.v. Asked if user wants testbench specs added to TODO.md.

  USER: yes add all three sections 1,2 and 3

  CLAUDE: Updated TODO.md verification section with detailed specifications for all
  three levels:
  - Level 1: Synthesis commands for each module
  - Level 2: Testbench specs for uart_tx_tb, uart_rx_tb, spi_master_tb (marked
  CRITICAL), i2c_master_tb with test cases and simulated slave models
  - Level 3: Hardware test project structure with 4 test configurations (UART
  loopback, I2C ADS1115, SPI DAC-ADC loopback, logic analyzer verification)

  USER: summarize this conversation with bullet points

